  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nSunnier skies are expected this afternoon with high temperatures \nrunning near to up to 7 degrees warmer than yesterday. The marine \nlayer is expected to get shallower over the next few nights, staying \nmostly confined the the coast. Conditions will warm through the \nmiddle of the week.\n\nWednesday, high temperatures will warm 5 to 12 degrees for the \nvalleys, with 3 to 5 degrees of warming elsewhere. Additional \nwarming is expected through Saturday with temperatures along the \ncoast with the highest departure from normal at 7 to 12 degrees. For \nthe latter half of the week, overnight lows will increase to the mid-\n60s to 70 west of the mountains and in the upper 70s to low 80s in \nthe low desert. Moderate HeatRisk is expected along the coast Friday \nand Saturday and in the low deserts for the second half of the week. \nModerate HeatRisk has the most impacts on those sensitive to heat, \nespecially those without cooling/hydration. A low pressure system \nis expected to move inland early next week which will bring \ncooling conditions and a deeper marine layer.\n\nFor Thursday, a closed low lingering to our southwest will start to \nmove east. This will draw moisture into Southern California from \nthe southeast increasing chances for precipitation, mainly in the \nmountains. There remains a 10% chance of thunderstorms over the \nmountains. \n\nTropical Cyclone Polo, currently off the southwest coast of \nMexico, is now a major hurricane. Polo is basically stationary but\nis expected to move generally west-northwest over the next few \ndays. There remains a lot of uncertainty in the forecast for late \nin the weekend into next week but there could eventually be at \nleast some peripheral impacts to portions of Southern California. \nRight now, it looks like the most likely impacts will be coastal \nflooding and erosion but that will ultimately depend on the track,\nintensity, and duration of the cyclone.",
